<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Bonaparte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a>–<a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Bonaparte was a younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, who made him king of Holland in 1806. Louis learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and tried to protect Dutch interests, which brought him into conflict with his brother. He abdicated in 1810, and Holland was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte.jpg" alt="Louis Bonaparte" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Howard Hodges · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LouisBonaparte_Holland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born in Ajaccio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, on 2 September 1778, the fifth surviving child and fourth surviving son of Carlo Buonaparte and Letizia Ramolino. Following his older brothers into the French Army, he benefited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s patronage, served in the campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and was a general by the age of 25, though he felt he had risen too high too fast. On 4 January 1802 he married Hortense de Beauharnais, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephine-de-beauharnais">Empress Joséphine</a>, a match arranged by Napoleon. Hortense at first opposed it and the couple never got on.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 5 June 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> replaced the Batavian Commonwealth with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a> and placed Louis on its throne, intending little more than a French prefect. Louis tried instead to rule independently. He called himself Lodewijk I, declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and learned Dutch, though allegedly so badly that at first he called himself the Konijn van 'Olland, the Rabbit of 'Olland, instead of the Koning van Holland. He required his court and ministers to speak only Dutch, and he changed capitals more than a dozen times, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> and Utrecht.</p><p>He oversaw relief after two disasters, the explosion of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> ship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> in 1807 and a flood in 1809, and was known as Louis the Good. Napoleon remarked that to call a king good meant he had failed in his rule. Their quarrel deepened when Napoleon wanted to cut the value of French loans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> investors by two thirds and to draw Dutch troops for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which Louis refused. After the British landed 40,000 men in 1809 and French militia drove them off, Napoleon cited Louis's inability to defend Holland. Louis abdicated on 1 July 1810 in favour of his second son, Napoleon Louis, and Holland was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the Decree of Rambouillet on 9 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louis took the title Count of Saint-Leu and found refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, between 1811 and 1813, where he wrote and composed poetry. King William I refused his requests to visit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, but under William II he travelled there in 1840 under a false name and was cheered by a crowd outside his hotel. After the death of his brother Joseph in 1844 the Bonapartists saw him as the rightful emperor, although he did little to press the claim. He died of a stroke on 25 July 1846 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> and was buried at Saint-Leu-la-Forêt. His youngest son, Louis-Napoléon, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-empire">Second French Empire</a> in 1852 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis Bonaparte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herman Willem Daendels, appointed governor-general by <b>Louis Bonaparte</b>’s Kingdom of Holland, arrived in 1808, centralised the administration of Java, curbed the Javanese courts and built the Great Post Road along the north coast with forced labour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon, wanting firmer control, turned the republic into the Kingdom of Holland in 1806 under his brother <b>Louis Bonaparte</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1806 Napoleon replaced the Batavian Republic with a kingdom and installed his brother <b>Louis Bonaparte</b> as king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ajaccio, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.93,8.74&amp;year=1822" aria-label="Ajaccio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louis Bonaparte"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5554 3966h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5554 3966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.93,8.74&amp;year=1822">What was here in 1822? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
